<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/"
      type="topic" id="getting-started">
  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#start"/>
    <title type="sort">1</title>
    <link type="seealso" xref="canvas"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="printing"/>
    <desc>Set up the printer, design a label, save it and print it.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Design and print a first label</title>

  <steps>
    <item>
      <p>Tell LinuxZPL where the printer is:
      <guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Default Printer…</gui></guiseq>. Type
      its network <gui>Address</gui> and <gui>Port</gui> (Zebra printers
      listen on 9100), then press <gui>Test Connection</gui>, which also asks
      the printer its resolution and fills in <gui>DPI</gui>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>Set the label's size:
      <guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Label Size…</gui></guiseq>. Pick one of
      the sizes listed, or type a width and height in inches.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>Add things to the label with the buttons down the left of the
      canvas: <gui>+ Text</gui>, <gui>+ Frame</gui>, <gui>+ Barcode</gui>,
      <gui>+ Image</gui> and the rest. Click a button to add at a spot of its
      own, or drag it onto the canvas to drop the new element where you
      want it.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>Drag an element to move it, or drag its handles to resize it. The
      <gui>Properties</gui> panel beside the canvas shows whatever is
      selected: type a new text, size or barcode value there and the canvas
      follows as you type.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>Save the label with <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Save</gui></guiseq>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>S</key></keyseq>). Labels are saved as ZPL,
      in <file>.zpl</file> files any Zebra printer can be sent.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <p>Print it with <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Print…</gui></guiseq>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>P</key></keyseq>), which lets you choose
      the copies, the darkness and the speed first, or with
      <guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Quick Print</gui></guiseq>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>P</key></keyseq>), which
      sends it as it is.</p>
    </item>
  </steps>

  <note style="tip">
    <p>Every window has a <gui>Help</gui> button that opens the page about
    that window, and <key>F1</key> in a window does the same. Anywhere else,
    <key>F1</key> opens the contents of this help.</p>
  </note>

  <section id="files">
    <title>Open and save labels</title>
    <list>
      <item><p><guiseq><gui>File</gui><gui>Open…</gui></guiseq>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>O</key></keyseq>) opens any
      <file>.zpl</file> file, and shows a picture of the label as you pick
      one. <gui>Open Recent</gui> lists the last ten files opened or
      saved.</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Save as…</gui>
      (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>S</key></keyseq>) asks for
      a name, and adds <file>.zpl</file> to a name typed without
      one.</p></item>
      <item><p>Before a new or opened label replaces unsaved changes, or
      LinuxZPL closes with some, it asks whether to save them.</p></item>
      <item><p>The file is plain ZPL. What only the designer needs, such as
      groups and the resolution the label was made for, goes in comments
      that printers ignore, so the file prints the same anywhere.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="canvas-is-proof">
    <title>The canvas shows what will print</title>
    <list>
      <item><p>Everything is measured in the printer's dots. A 4 × 6 inch
      label is 812 × 1218 dots on a 203 dpi printer.</p></item>
      <item><p>A thermal printer only adds black; it cannot erase. An image
      placed over text does not hide the text, which prints through the
      image's white parts, and the canvas shows it that way.</p></item>
      <item><p>Images are shown as the printer receives them: shrunk or
      enlarged to their box and turned into black and white dots.</p></item>
      <item><p>Text is drawn at the width it will print, in the font it will
      print in.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>
</page>
